// expect-error: E003
// CVE Retrofit #01: CVE-2021-44228 — Log4Shell (Apache Log4j JNDI injection)
// Scope: STRUCTURAL
//
// ORIGINAL VULNERABILITY
//   Apache Log4j 2.x performed JNDI lookups when log messages contained
//   `${jndi:...}` substrings. An attacker who controlled any string
//   reaching the logger (e.g., User-Agent header) could trigger the
//   server to fetch and execute arbitrary code over LDAP/RMI. CVSS 10.0.
//
// SIGIL RETROFIT
//   In SIGIL, inner-ring (default) modules cannot declare functions
//   carrying `FFI` or `Unsafe` effects. A logger that secretly performs
//   network fetches must declare those effects in its row; the effect
//   checker fires E003 against the function in inner ring.
//
// Why this resembles the original: Log4Shell existed because a logger
// (intuitively a pure-write operation) had hidden FFI authority for
// JNDI lookups. SIGIL refuses the "logger silently does I/O" pattern
// at the declaration site, before any malicious payload can be crafted.
//
// MUTATION CONTRACT
//   MUTATION_SITE: the single line declaring an inner-ring function
//   with `FFI, Unsafe` effects. Removing it leaves only a pure
//   inner-ring `safe_log` function; the program compiles cleanly.
module cve;

fn safe_log(msg_ptr: i64) -> i64 ! { Alloc } { let buf: i64 = alloc(msg_ptr); return buf; }
fn bad_log(msg_ptr: i64) -> i64 ! { FFI, Unsafe } { return msg_ptr; }  // MUTATION_SITE
